00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.820
before the camera started
rolling about we're planning

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:37.700
for the exchange of ideas and well

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:40.623
I will be on the Rich Roll
Podcast soon, we're scheming.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.090
It's a super treat to have you on the show

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:47.660
and I also have noticed
your hard work from afar.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.770
Starting one of the
first things I realized

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.140
was when you ran five Ironmans

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:54.270
or you did because it's more than running

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.020
there is biking and swimming.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.020
Five in, was it five or seven
days or something like that

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.100
in Hawaii on all the different islands?

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.210
- I did this thing...

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:03.778
- This is freakish by the way.

00:02:03.778 --> 00:02:04.611
- Yeah.

00:02:04.611 --> 00:02:06.820
- This is no like, this is--
- It was a while ago.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:11.560
Well it was in 2010 so the
story is my buddy Jason Lester

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:14.170
who's an incredible endurance athlete,

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:16.030
an inspirational figure.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.350
A training partner of mine from many,

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:20.690
an Ultraman competition,

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:23.280
I would do these double
Ironman competitions.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.300
He came up with this harebrained scheme,

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.010
this idea to try to complete five Ironmans

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.410
on five Hawaiian Islands in five days.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.260
Literally an Ironman a day,

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:35.110
traveling island to island to island.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:38.600
- Tell people who don't know
what an Ironman actually is.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.040
- Yeah, for those that don't know

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:44.150
an Ironman a very long triathlon.

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.760
Widely considered the ultimate
test of human endurance.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.690
Where in over the course of
one day, you swim 2.4 miles.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:55.390
You ride your bike 112
miles and then you celebrate

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:59.050
that by running a marathon, all at once.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:01.039
Super difficult and hard.

00:03:01.039 --> 00:03:01.872
- And you wanted to stack five of those?

00:03:01.872 --> 00:03:04.880
- Jesus, decided we should
do five of those in a row

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.950
and in between get on airplanes

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:09.500
and try to schlep our gear from
island to island to island.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.397
So we took a stab at it in 2010

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.410
on May 5th, I might add.

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.000
So it was five five

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.050
on the day that we started it.
- Wow, bam bam.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:24.080
- We ran into all kinds of
crazy logistical problems.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:27.250
It was quite the in
certain respects a debacle

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:29.710
but also an amazing
challenge and adventure.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.470
So it took us a little
bit longer than five days

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.523
but we got it done, it was crazy.

00:03:35.124 --> 00:03:36.540
- You're making it sound
like it was two years

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.910
but it was actually like seven days or--

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.500
- It was like six and a half days.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.510
- Yeah I mean we were
like losing bike parts,

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:46.960
like we had some people,
some beautiful people

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.400
who volunteered to help us.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:49.233
- [Chase] Yeah.

00:03:49.233 --> 00:03:52.110
- But we didn't have a
whole crew situation dollar

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.300
we didn't know we were doing
- Yeah, infrastructure.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.140
No private flights.
- The crazy thing was that

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.760
the logistics were just as challenging,

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.350
it was almost a relief to just
be out on your bike doing it

00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:03.590
because we knew we have
to finish by this time

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:06.460
because the last plane leaves the island.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:07.477
And we have to get on that

00:04:07.477 --> 00:04:08.930
and we have to bring all our gear,

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:10.760
and then we would get to the next island

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:12.080
and have to get to our hotel.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.160
And by the time we ate and took a shower

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:16.660
it was the middle of the night

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:18.150
and we had two or three hours of sleep.

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:20.880
So the sleep deprivation
started to catch up to us

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.720
and we're, I mean it was
bananas the whole thing that.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:27.090
- I remember that being widely reported

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:28.010
- Yeah.
- And oh my God

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:29.260
who is this guy?
- Right.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:31.470
- I gotta meet this character man.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:32.870
So and here we are

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:35.160
whatever years later.
- We're here.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.889
- Well A, thanks for starting off

00:04:36.889 --> 00:04:40.030
with the kind words around content.

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.540
You have been putting out a ton

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.363
of high-quality content as well.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.610
The areas of focus that
I wanna talk about today

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:51.410
that it's my goal to assemble
world-class performers

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:53.413
from a whole bunch of disciplines.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:58.153
And you have both achieved

00:04:58.153 --> 00:05:00.930
an amazing physical feats,

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:04.260
you've also had built a
really interesting business.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.803
But mostly your ideas
around what's possible,

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:12.240
see the story we just talked about,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.363
plant-based diet something
I'm fascinated by.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:21.320
The storytelling of your book.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:22.440
And I think you have two books, right?

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:23.670
You have a cookbook that you did

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:24.610
with your wife is that right?

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:25.690
Or sort of a plant-based,

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:27.200
you've got your memoir.
- We've got yeah.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:28.990
I wrote a memoir called Finding Ultra,

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:31.100
and then we have three cookbooks actually.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.850
- Three cookbooks, cool.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:36.550
Not being an only
plant-based eater I have not,

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:37.890
my confession here for you

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:41.320
is that I have not consumed the cookbooks.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:44.860
But Finding Ultra is an
amazing narrative of your life

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:47.660
and it's not just about plant-based diet,

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:48.710
it's about your life.

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:53.473
So I'd like to start, let's
go back in time we can.

00:05:55.370 --> 00:06:00.370
You're an endurance athlete,
what made you want to be that?

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:04.070
What part of you, this is a
strange part of the human brain.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:05.450
- Yeah.
- That clicks into saying,

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:09.830
I wanna run a marathon plus 120 mile

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:12.230
plus a marathon, bike 120 miles

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:15.380
and swim two and a half, that's nuts.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:17.530
And you've done crazier
stuff too by the way.

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:18.550
- Yeah I've done a bunch of stuff.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.820
I mean I look at it all,

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:23.500
not to put too woo of a point on it

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:26.130
but for me it's like
all a spiritual journey

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:31.040
of personal growth, and
these crazy endurance races

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.930
are like a vehicle for self-discovery.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:36.930
In the same way that an
artist might find that

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:39.610
in a photograph or in
pursuing a photograph

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.420
or a painting or making a film.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:45.950
The medium for me is the outer limits

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:47.620
of what my body is capable of.

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:49.190
And I think it was originally

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.300
inspired by a couple things.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.430
First of all I was a swimmer in college.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:58.120
So I had a background as an athlete,

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:00.920
I swam at Stanford in the late 1980s.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.240
We won two NCAA Championships
when I was there.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.670
I was a benchwarmer I was by no means

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:09.370
a big point scorer or star on that team,

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:12.293
but I competed as an athlete
at a very high level.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:15.650
Drugs and alcohol destroyed that career

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:18.350
and it really cut the head off

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:20.200
me achieving my potential as an athlete.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:24.410
So I feel like later in life I
still had unfinished business

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:28.600
that I wanted to pursue to see
what I would be capable of.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:33.600
And also athletics aside

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:38.270
when I hit around 40, 41
I was having a little bit

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:39.550
of an existential crisis

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:41.350
about what I was doing with my life.

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:44.000
There's a longer story baked into that

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.300
but basically I've been
pursuing this career in law.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:48.580
I was on the partnership track

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:50.650
at a big prestigious law firm.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:51.800
- Went to law at Cornell.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.163
- I went to law at Cornell.

00:07:54.450 --> 00:07:56.570
In the wake of problems
with drugs and alcohol

00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:59.740
I got sober at 31 and then
spent the next nine years

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:01.750
trying to repair all the
wreckage that I'd created

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:03.890
as a result of my drinking and using,

00:08:03.890 --> 00:08:07.940
was successful in that regard
and was very much blindly

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:12.100
just looking at that traditional

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.030
equation of success.

00:08:14.030 --> 00:08:17.050
Partnership, car, house, brass ring,

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:18.560
the whole like traditional notion

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.170
of what it means to be a
successful male in America.

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:24.003
- [Chase] Yeah.

00:08:24.003 --> 00:08:27.490
- And played that out to
the point where I realized

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:30.700
that there was some emptiness in that.

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:33.340
And had this confused state

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:34.673
about what I was doing with myself

00:08:34.673 --> 00:08:37.150
because I wasn't doing it out of a passion

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:39.320
for the law or any of that,

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:41.017
I was doing it to prove to myself

00:08:41.017 --> 00:08:43.010
and to others that I
could be a responsible,

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:45.220
respectful person after being

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.900
such a train wreck for so many years.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:52.180
And I think trying to process
what that meant for myself

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:56.590
created this this sense of confusion.

00:08:56.590 --> 00:09:01.590
And I found myself back into fitness

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:06.270
as a way of spending time
alone to process all of this,

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:07.750
to try to find a way forward.

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:10.020
There was never some conscious decision

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:12.490
that I was gonna become
this competitive athlete

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:15.979
in my forties and go back and
revisit unfinished business.

00:09:15.979 --> 00:09:16.812
- [Chase] Yeah.

00:09:16.812 --> 00:09:19.380
- It was really an organic
outgrowth out of trying to,

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:22.230
out of wrestling with and
trying to answer these questions

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:25.273
for myself about what I
was gonna do with my life.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.210
- Now I would...

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:32.220
Is there a relationship
between your addiction

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:34.480
to drugs and alcohol and your passion,

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.400
would you call it an
addiction to exercise.

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:38.290
Was it, were trading one thing for,

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:40.560
a destructive thing for a healthy thing?

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:43.270
Or was there a reconciliation process

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:46.700
'cause I think trauma and
we all have trauma in life

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:48.410
and we all expressed it in different ways,

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:51.760
and I'm just curious how

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:53.660
you thought about it.
- Yeah.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:55.403
I think it's a little bit of both.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.800
What you find in the
ultra-endurance community

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:02.670
is a lot of people who are in recovery

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:07.670
from some form of abuse
or substance addiction.

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:13.940
And I think it would be
intellectually dishonest to say no.

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:15.530
I was addicted to drugs and alcohol

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:16.770
but my relationship with these,

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:19.120
doing these crazy things
is completely healthy.

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:22.842
Of course there's some
compulsive aspect to that.

00:10:22.842 --> 00:10:24.190
- [Chase] Yeah.

00:10:24.190 --> 00:10:29.190
- But I found my healthier self

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:31.110
through those pursuits.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:36.050
Like for me taking the drink
was always the easy way out.

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:38.970
And getting up in the morning
to put your running shoes on

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:43.090
or staring down the barrel of
some crazy difficult workout,

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:44.520
that's a harder choice.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:47.700
That requires a little bit
of backbone and character.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.590
So for me it's been a learning experience

00:10:50.590 --> 00:10:52.070
and it's been a positive thing.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:56.920
But I do have to keep
those tendencies in check.

00:10:56.920 --> 00:10:59.320
I'm married, I've got four kids like--

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:01.350
- Four? Congrats.
- Left unchecked,

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:04.140
like I'll move into a herd
and just train all day.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:05.810
And just think about myself.

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:10.770
Like I'm naturally a
selfish self-seeking person.

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:15.770
And recovery for me is
about balancing that out

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:19.630
against everything else in my life,

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:20.780
that's truly important.

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:23.040
So the pendulum is always swinging

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:25.600
and I always have to kind
of be doing an inventory

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:29.520
about how I'm relating to the world

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:31.830
and is that in a healthy way

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:34.610
or is that in a very
unhealthy or compulsive way.

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:36.960
- I love that you said
unfinished business.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:39.810
I think that it's a beautiful descriptor,

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:44.810
and do you feel that
you've been able to redeem

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:48.193
some of that business
that was that was undone?

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:52.670
- Certainly I mean now I look back on it,

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:54.390
and the idea that I needed

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:57.010
to redeem anything is sort of silly.

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:58.680
But certainly I have done that,

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.760
I mean I've been more successful

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:04.530
and more lauded for things
I've done athletically

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:09.070
than I ever was when I was
truly at my athletic peak.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:09.903
- [Chase] Yeah.

00:12:09.903 --> 00:12:12.260
- So that's been
fascinating and unexpected

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:14.660
and completely bizarre.

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:18.360
I got into these events like I said

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:20.980
as a sort of spiritual odyssey

00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:24.270
to learn more about what made me tick.

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:27.670
The fact that anybody
else would care about that

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:32.030
other than maybe my wife
has been a strange journey.

00:12:32.030 --> 00:12:33.590
And a story I'm happy to now tell

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:37.490
but one that was not intended
and very much unexpected.

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:38.490
- Well let's keep telling it.

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:42.160
So you, there's this desire to pursue

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:44.170
some spiritual awakening,

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:46.400
40s you can call it a midlife crisis.

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:50.540
Why I wanna pull on this is,
it's not not a surprise here.

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:53.840
There are a lot of people
who have pursued a path,

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:57.560
often the path whether
it was law or medicine

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:02.150
or doing what your father or
mother did because it was easy.

00:13:02.150 --> 00:13:03.280
There's a lot of people who are listening

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:05.580
and watching right now,
who are playing this out

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.020
and saying, oh my gosh this is me.

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:10.380
I'm in the middle of my life
and thinking, what have I done?

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:13.110
I've been living the
dream than somebody else

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:15.810
had for me, I'm pursuing
somebody else's script

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:17.170
it's time I gotta write my own.

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:20.968
So share with us your version.

00:13:20.968 --> 00:13:23.310
How do you reconcile those things

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:26.550
and get started with the next chapter.

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:29.470
- I wish I could give you
the three bullet points.

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:30.303
Here's how you do it.
- There is three points

00:13:30.303 --> 00:13:31.163
you need to know kids.

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:36.910
- For me it was a long
drawn-out painful experience.

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:39.780
And like I said getting out of my bike

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:41.560
or getting out on the trail to run

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:43.660
or jumping in the pool or the ocean,

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:47.373
were almost ways of,

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:52.980
of connecting with myself the
in the best way that I could.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:55.370
Like that time alone.

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:58.180
I wasn't at the time an active meditator,

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:01.220
and I wouldn't say that
those training sessions

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:05.400
were meditation proper but
they were an active meditation

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:08.660
that allowed me to connect
with myself in a deeper way.

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:10.950
In a way that I wasn't used to

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:12.950
that allowed me the space and the time

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:17.950
and the bandwidth to really
sit with my discontent

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:22.300
and really ponder what a
future might look like.

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:25.300
And what I get to do
now was not the result

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:28.550
of some grand scheme that I
concocted while out on the bike,

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:30.970
it's really been an organic outgrowth

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:34.470
of me making it a fundamental decision

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:38.456
to just start doing what felt right to me.

00:14:38.456 --> 00:14:39.289
- [Chase] Yeah.

00:14:39.289 --> 00:14:41.060
- To follow my instincts in my intuition

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:43.330
irrespective of whatever
the peanut gallery

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:46.820
had to say about it or whether society

00:14:46.820 --> 00:14:50.920
was gonna launder me with
social approval or not.

00:14:50.920 --> 00:14:53.621
To just say, look I don't care.

00:14:53.621 --> 00:14:56.620
I like what it feels
like to jump in the pool.

00:14:56.620 --> 00:15:00.750
I like what it feels like to
have the sun on my shoulder

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:05.750
on a trail run at dawn and I'm
gonna just keep doing that,

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:10.490
in a very childlike way, it's like play.

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:13.370
And as we grow up we're taught,

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:15.800
well this is things we don't do any more.

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:17.880
This is mature, respectful adult.

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:19.280
- Immature or not responsible.

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:23.390
- Exactly and I just decided
that I was gonna do it.

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:24.800
And I was gonna listen to that music

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:26.860
and I was gonna entertain it.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:29.200
Not because I thought that I would find

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:34.200
some career path baked into
this, but just because.

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:37.800
And I think by continuing
to double down on that

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:41.530
ultimately the path has
sort of been laid forward

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:43.680
in front of me, brick by brick

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:46.020
to figure out what to do next.

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:49.000
But it's been very
difficult like financially

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.240
incredibly insecure we
almost lost our house.

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:52.740
I've had cars repossessed,

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:56.734
like it was not this upward trajectory

00:15:56.734 --> 00:16:00.080
to having this podcast and getting to sit

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:01.650
and talk to you today.

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:04.030
There were a lot of
dark moments of the soul

00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:08.420
and a lot of questioning
whether I was an insane person.

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:10.712
Because everyone around me
was saying what are you doing?

00:16:10.712 --> 00:16:12.440
You could be practicing law,

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:13.400
you could be doing all these things,

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:14.550
you could be making all this money

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:16.690
like what you're chasing
this crazy fool's errand,

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:19.210
you have kids, you're being irresponsible.

00:16:19.210 --> 00:16:21.540
So it took a lot of faith

00:16:23.735 --> 00:16:26.070
and really conviction in order

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:28.077
to kind of stay true to the path.

00:16:28.077 --> 00:16:30.610
- Was that a muscle that you'd always had?

00:16:30.610 --> 00:16:32.100
- No, definitely not.
- Or was that a muscle

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:33.370
that you had to develop?

00:16:33.370 --> 00:16:37.380
And if you had to develop
it what was your process?

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:39.790
- Definitely not a
muscle that I always had.

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:41.960
I wouldn't consider myself

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:44.740
an entrepreneur type personality.

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:47.610
I was reared very much as a safety seeker,

00:16:47.610 --> 00:16:49.700
like just get into the best school

00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:53.915
and like take that path,
that well trodden path.

00:16:53.915 --> 00:16:57.010
That is laid out for men,

00:16:57.010 --> 00:16:59.230
respectable men of your pedigree.

00:16:59.230 --> 00:17:01.680
Like I grew up upper-middle class,

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:03.110
I went to really nice schools.

00:17:03.110 --> 00:17:05.240
I have parents that took
care of all of my needs

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:07.750
that are still together,
I wanted for nothing

00:17:07.750 --> 00:17:09.560
and this was kind of the trajectory

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:11.280
that all my peers were on.

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:13.770
It never occurred to
me to veer off of that

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:17.650
until I was really like almost soul sick.

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:21.623
And felt like I had no
other choice but to do that.

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:25.190
So for me it was very unfamiliar.

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:26.770
It was almost like I had to shed

00:17:26.770 --> 00:17:28.570
a skin and grow a new one.

00:17:28.570 --> 00:17:32.750
And my process was hardly methodical

00:17:32.750 --> 00:17:37.750
but I would say that my wife really

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:40.190
was my greatest teacher
through this process.

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:43.130
Like she just had my back even when things

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:44.757
just looked crazy.
- That's so powerful.

00:17:44.757 --> 00:17:47.650
She was like, and I was like,
I gotta go make some money

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:48.780
like the way that I know how to.

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:52.337
She'd say, "No, like
you need to keep doing

00:17:52.337 --> 00:17:54.787
"what you're doing, we
will figure this out.

00:17:54.787 --> 00:17:57.387
"But the path forward and the way through

00:17:57.387 --> 00:18:00.377
"all of these difficulties
is not to retreat

00:18:00.377 --> 00:18:02.327
"and trace your steps backwards

00:18:02.327 --> 00:18:04.000
"but to continue to blaze forward."

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:08.100
And I think without that
support in the home,

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:10.410
I don't know that I would
have been able to keep going.

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:14.360
So great credit to her.
- Do you think is,

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:18.370
she important for you

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:21.440
as the person that she is

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:24.230
or is it important that when people

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:26.590
are trying to go in the same journey

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:29.150
that you've been through,
that there are people

00:18:29.150 --> 00:18:31.220
that are in their corner.

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:34.133
Is that mentors, is it a
partner, is it a buddy?

00:18:34.133 --> 00:18:34.966
- [Rich] Yeah.

00:18:34.966 --> 00:18:35.930
- Of course ideally it's your spouse

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:37.820
because it sounds like she's she was,

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:39.620
is very powerful force for you.

00:18:39.620 --> 00:18:41.010
But I'm thinking to everyone that doesn't,

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:41.870
what's your wife's name?

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:42.703
- [Rich] Julie.

00:18:42.703 --> 00:18:45.710
- Okay to everybody who
doesn't have a Julie at home,

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:47.097
can you give a--

00:18:47.097 --> 00:18:49.460
- I think it's super
critical and important

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:52.243
to find like-minded souls

00:18:52.243 --> 00:18:55.620
who support your vision for yourself

00:18:55.620 --> 00:19:00.620
and hold that space for your best self

00:19:01.270 --> 00:19:03.210
and believe in you.

00:19:03.210 --> 00:19:04.780
I think you need to balance that

00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:07.950
with other people who can
give you the hard feedback

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:11.160
about when you're going
off-track or being a crazy person

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:12.960
who can give you the hard truth.

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:16.640
'Cause cheerleaders alone
are not gonna get you there,

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:18.550
you need both.

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:21.880
You need the tension
between the aspiration

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:24.390
and the grounded reality
in order to find yourself.

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:26.880
And I think if you, I think we all need,

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:31.250
I think the word mentor
I find to be problematic

00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:33.220
because there's a formality to it.

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:36.500
And everyone chases the fancy name people,

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:39.290
I want you to be my
mentor I think everybody

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:44.040
has people in their community who can be

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:50.420
a source of truth and education for--

00:19:50.462 --> 00:19:51.295
- And if you don't you gotta

00:19:51.295 --> 00:19:52.760
find those people right away.
- Yeah right, you should find,

00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:53.593
seek them out.

00:19:53.593 --> 00:19:55.230
They are all around you,

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:56.950
and if you can't find
them in your neighborhood

00:19:56.950 --> 00:19:58.620
you can find them online and you can have

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:00.780
a virtual relationship with somebody.

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:02.080
But I think it's super important

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:04.050
to have a board of directors.

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:06.580
I have people in my life
as I'm sure you do too

00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:10.419
that I call and rely
upon for every facet of--

00:20:10.419 --> 00:20:12.092
- [Chase] I was on the
phone this morning--

00:20:12.092 --> 00:20:16.530
- Whether it's marriage or
parenting or profession,

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:17.930
all of these things.

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:19.530
I think it's important
to surround yourself

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:23.170
with people who are living the life

00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:26.117
that you aspire to have
in certain categories.

00:20:26.117 --> 00:20:28.980
And are further down the path in than you.

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:30.990
- You found both the tough love

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:33.370
and the cheerleader in Julie?

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:34.520
- Yeah.
- Sounds like.

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:37.860
Tell me what are some of the things

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.560
that you heard from her
that were surprising.

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:43.120
You just shared one which is like,

00:20:43.120 --> 00:20:44.480
now is not the time to retreat,

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:45.950
now it's the time to go through.

00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:46.783
- [Rich] Right.

00:20:46.783 --> 00:20:48.060
- What were some other,
was there some themes

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:50.830
or some commonalities that you were like,

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:53.500
okay she's got my back?

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:56.010
This is what she said this
to me four or five times now

00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:57.963
and I know, fill in the black.

00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:02.610
- We were just getting
crushed financially.

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:06.880
I mean it was, looking back on
it now, it's almost comical.

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:08.360
Like we--

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:10.490
- It's only comical because
you came back on it, right?

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:12.480
Exactly because we're
on the other side of it,

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:14.470
but like I said like cars repossessed

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:17.870
and we had our trash bins taken away.

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:20.753
I mean really like embarrassing,
demoralizing stuff.

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:26.670
And what Julie taught me

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:27.820
through this whole process

00:21:27.820 --> 00:21:30.313
was how to maintain your equanimity.

00:21:31.190 --> 00:21:35.790
She would say your job is
to be completely neutral

00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:39.050
like can you, because that
is like the Jedi path, right?

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:42.640
That is like true power to be a warrior

00:21:42.640 --> 00:21:44.780
in the eye of a storm like that

00:21:44.780 --> 00:21:47.100
when everything's coming
at you and you feel like

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:48.800
life is out of control.

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:52.020
If you can be neutral and
maintain your integrity,

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:54.630
your peace of mind your equanimity,

00:21:54.630 --> 00:21:56.190
that's like a superpower.

00:21:56.190 --> 00:21:59.210
So that was something
that I observed in her

00:21:59.210 --> 00:22:02.050
that I tried to hone within myself.

00:22:02.050 --> 00:22:05.750
I remember when the repo guy
came to take my truck away,

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:07.860
and she came out and
greeted him in the driveway.

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:10.427
And she said, "Oh hey I'm Julie.

00:22:10.427 --> 00:22:12.340
"Would you like some tea,
do you want to come in?"

00:22:12.340 --> 00:22:16.390
She was super nice to him
and he was so confused.

00:22:16.390 --> 00:22:17.820
He's like what?
- He's like, wait a minute?

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:20.110
- Yeah I know so--

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:20.943
- Are they're gonna murder me

00:22:20.943 --> 00:22:22.580
if I go there and have
tea with these people,

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:23.870
who are these crazies?

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:25.377
- The other thing,

00:22:25.377 --> 00:22:26.230
- It's amazing.
- Is to understand

00:22:26.230 --> 00:22:27.680
that if you're being visited

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:31.473
with these kinds of
difficulties in your life.

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:36.240
To detach from the notion
that this is failure

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:39.520
or a referendum on who
you are as a person,

00:22:39.520 --> 00:22:42.630
but rather to look upon
it as an opportunity.

00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:46.460
This is your divine moment
we are here on earth to grow,

00:22:46.460 --> 00:22:51.320
to expand and these are lessons for us.

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:53.530
So our job is to pay attention

00:22:53.530 --> 00:22:55.850
and to find the nugget
of wisdom within that

00:22:55.850 --> 00:23:00.850
and to grab onto that and
figure out the path forward.

00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:01.843
- Wow.
- Yeah.

00:23:02.820 --> 00:23:07.080
- So I can't overstate

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:14.580
the thread throughout the
hundreds of interviews

00:23:14.580 --> 00:23:17.310
that I've personally done
with you and folks like you

00:23:17.310 --> 00:23:19.290
and and others that have listened to

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:23.130
on your show and others,
that that having community,

00:23:23.130 --> 00:23:24.530
a few or the average of the five people

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:26.430
you spend the most time with.

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:28.800
You looked around and looked at who

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:30.190
you're spending time with I think that is

00:23:30.190 --> 00:23:31.650
such a common thread.

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:33.550
I also will jump on the same bandwagon

00:23:33.550 --> 00:23:35.170
like the whole mentor thing

00:23:35.170 --> 00:23:36.280
now you have people asking me to be

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:37.440
their mentor all the time.

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:40.850
And it's just like sorry
I don't even know you

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:44.600
but here's a community
and creating a community

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:47.010
and a community atmosphere
where you can learn

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:49.740
the fact that we're in a world where

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:52.263
this information is available for free.

00:23:53.240 --> 00:23:55.620
To me I think that's
just I wanna restate that

00:23:55.620 --> 00:23:57.790
because I think you said it
much more eloquently than I did

00:23:57.790 --> 00:23:59.640
but it's really, really powerful

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:02.360
to have there not necessarily a person

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:07.130
but just a set of people
that you are inspired by,

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:10.300
that you respond to,
that you want to emulate.

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:13.550
And the question I have out of all this is

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:16.390
if you're around these people,

00:24:16.390 --> 00:24:19.300
they're all have their own identities

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:22.190
and you're learning
from this person online

00:24:22.190 --> 00:24:24.110
or this person in your life,

00:24:24.110 --> 00:24:28.410
how did you find the part
of you in all of that?

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:31.920
How did you not just become
a reflection of Julie

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:34.010
if she was your rock in this time.

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:36.550
How did you find you?

00:24:36.550 --> 00:24:40.023
- Wow that's a great question,
that's a hard question.

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:42.303
- [Chase] It's my job.

00:24:42.303 --> 00:24:43.550
I mean I think I'm...
(laughs)

00:24:43.550 --> 00:24:45.023
Yeah, that's a good one.

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:48.630
I think I'm still trying to find that.

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:50.930
I don't know that I have
arrived in that place,

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:53.420
I don't know if anybody
truly arrives in that place.

00:24:53.420 --> 00:24:55.220
Like if I had to say Chase

00:24:55.220 --> 00:24:57.910
who are you, defined yourself?
- Don't do this.

00:24:57.910 --> 00:24:58.743
I said you're such a such a great guest

